            "overlay_texts": [
                "Kael moves through the Conservatory&#039;s Luminara Gardens with a stack of student dissertations pressed against his chest, his black tactical military-style vest with carbon fiber texture catching fragments of prismatic afternoon light as emerald vines part instinctively to let him passhis heterochromia eyes one red-brown human and one glowing bright green tracking the botanical recognition, white messy swept-back hair with green vine-like strands intertwined catching the filtered green light, moss and plant growth spreading across his left side of face and jaw seamlessly blending with the foliage around him, his Vitalis partnership with the plants so refined that they recognize him before sight. His lecture on Root Rot containment still echoes in his mind, facts arranged with surgical precision, each data point a small truth that collectively obscured a greater one. Later, in the outer-ring greenhouse where humidity clings clammy and obscene against his skin, he kneels beside a planter labeled Pleocene Heritage, glowing cyan circuit board patterns on his right forearm gauntlet catching the amber grow-lights, green vines and roots overtaking his left arm and shoulder moving with deliberate control, his fingers working with choreographed casualnessthe old package withdrawn, the new one positioned, four seconds of perfect restraintthe bioluminescent glow from his chest-mounted electronic panel with small green indicator lights casting dramatic shadows across the soil as he works, and for a moment the olfactory burn of soil and decomposing mulch makes him contemplate the irony: he teaches students how to mend the jungle&#039;s wounds while secretly amplifying them. The walk home stretches long and temperate, his sharp angular facial features set in serious determined expression revealing nothing, military patches and insignia on his shoulders catching the fading light, and by the time he settles at his desk beneath a lofty window, grading papers with a chemist&#039;s precision, the divergence between Kael-the-scholar and Kael-the-instrument has become so naturalized it requires no rehearsaljust the constant unremarkable discipline of a man who has learned to live coherently across two incompatible truths. The glowing green cylindrical container with plant specimen sits at the desk&#039;s edge, bright green bioluminescent glow illuminating the papers before him, vignette shadows darkening the periphery, deep teal and dark navy laboratory atmosphere suffusing the mundane act of grading with existential tension."
            ],
